2026-09-18 | offline guarantee audit: findings reported, NOT fixed | Three gaps found outside the model-loading entry points, deliberately left alone as adjacent scope: (1) the Vulkan audio.cpp `.gguf` model is NOT in the offline bundle (`scripts/build_offline_bundle.py` has no audio.cpp references) and `the-oracle setup-vulkan` downloads it, so `--inference-backend vulkan` cannot resolve its model from the seeded cache on an offline install — the backend itself only loads a local file or fails, it never fetches. (2) the LanguageTool snapshot (hundreds of MB) is likewise not in the bundle; the grammar path is offline-safe in the sense that `_language_tool_download_ready()` is checked first and the render proceeds via the local fallback, but when the snapshot is absent `_warm_language_tool_download()` starts a background download, which an offline install would attempt. (3) `scripts/download_models.py` and `scripts/build_offline_bundle.py` intentionally do not honour the marker: their job is to fetch (prefetch / build the bundle), so honouring it would defeat them. Also honest about what did NOT break: the pinned-SHA loaders (the engine's `snapshot_download(revision=<pin>)` and the transformers helper models) resolve locally from a properly seeded cache even with offline mode off — measured against this machine's real hub cache — so the guarantee's value is no traffic at all on an air-gapped box, a fast clear failure instead of a 23s retry storm for anything the cache cannot satisfy, and never silently fetching a different revision. Recommended next pass: bundle the audio.cpp model (or make `setup-vulkan` detect the marker and refuse with a clear message), and skip the LanguageTool warm download when the marker is present.

